{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>Keep the day of Shabbat.</b> In the first set of utterances, which came to remind them of the day of Shabbat that He gave them at Marah, the term זכור (remember) applies. But here, in the second set of utterances, which were spoken in the fortieth year for the sake of those who had not heard the first ones, the term שמירה (keeping) applies, because the matter was not new to them. Another interpretation: <b>Keep the day of Shabbat</b> — in the sense of ואביו שמר את הדבר (and his father kept the matter): wait from before the day arrives — when will Shabbat come, to sanctify it? Like Rabbi Yannai, who would wrap himself and say: Come, bride; come, bride. Another interpretation: זכור (remember) was said for those who go down to the sea, and שמור (keep) for those who dwell on dry land." "<b>As He commanded you.</b> Rashi's explanation: at Marah. In the first utterances, זכור was stated, which refers to the Shabbat given at Marah — there was no need to write כאשר צוך (as He commanded you). But in these utterances, where זכור was not stated but rather שמור, it was necessary to write <b>as He commanded you</b>, to explain that it was at Marah, because both here and earlier He warned them about the commandment of Shabbat given at Marah, where the essence of the commandment was. And a proof for this matter: from the Passover Haggadah — 'Had He given us the Shabbat and not brought us before Mount Sinai,' etc. And according to the plain sense, <b>as He commanded you</b> — that is, just as He explained to you the reason in the first utterances: כי ששת ימים עשה ה׳ etc. Another interpretation: in the matter of שמור and in the matter of כבד, it is fitting to write כאשר צוך, because both of them are positive commandments."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:51:52.578918141Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}